INT. RETIREMENT HOME RECREATION HALL - DAY

A long room with a linoleum floor the color of weak tea. A bingo cage in one corner, a folding table of sad cookies in another. On the wall, a banner reads "SPRING FLING" and has read so since the previous spring.

Two people occupy the room, and a third is on her way.

MAXINE "MAX" OKONKWO-BELL, 70s, a retired stunt performer in a velvet tracksuit, sits at the center table with a deck of cards fanned in one hand. Beside her, a dozing oxygen tank on wheels and a bowl of butterscotch. Across from her, her grandson DESMOND, 20s, thin, pressed shirt, a lanyard that says VISITOR, reads a text message with his thumb stuck in his mouth.

A phone on the wall buzzes. Nobody answers it. It buzzes again.

MAX
Desmond, your telephone is ringing.

DESMOND
It's a text, Nana.

MAX
That's the wall phone.

DESMOND
Oh.

He glances at it. It buzzes. He looks at Max, who raises one eyebrow so high it nearly leaves her face. He sighs, crosses the room, and lifts the receiver. Max shuffles. The cards ride a bridge, snap, and settle. Desmond holds the phone out from his ear as a voice squawks.

FLO (V.O.)
(over the wall phone)
Good morning, Mr. Okonkwo-Bell. It's Flo at the front desk. Your grandmother has a delivery.

DESMOND
My grandmother has a what?

FLO (V.O.)
A delivery. I'm bringing it up myself.

DESMOND
(turning)
Nana, what did you order?

MAX
Nothing.

DESMOND
They say it's a delivery.

MAX
Then it's a surprise. Hang up.

DESMOND
It's from the front desk.

MAX
Then hang up on the front desk.

He hangs up. At once the double doors swing open and in backs the nurse's aide FLORENCE "FLO" ABERNATHY, 50s, teal scrubs, hair under a net, wheeling a crate the size of a refrigerator on a hand truck. She parks it in the middle of the floor, unstraps it, tips it forward, and peels away the cardboard. Beneath is a vintage motorcycle, gleaming chrome, a leather jacket draped across the seat.

Desmond walks around it twice. Flo wipes her hands on her scrubs. Max does not rise.

DESMOND
Nana.

MAX
Hm?

DESMOND
Is this yours?

MAX
Was.

DESMOND
Nana, you said you sold it in 1994.

MAX
I said a lot of things in 1994.

Flo approaches the bike, runs a thumb along the handlebar, and glances back at Max.

FLO
Miss Max, it's not on the activity calendar.

MAX
Flo, I will tell you what's on my calendar.

FLO
You're due for your pills at noon.

MAX
My calendar says I'm due for a ride at noon.

DESMOND
You cannot ride it.

MAX
I can sit on it.

DESMOND
You can't sit on it either. Dr. Pruitt specifically--

MAX
Dr. Pruitt isn't here, and the man on the bike, in the photograph over there, was the best looking thing in the state of Ohio.

She nods at a framed photograph on the wall, a younger Max in a leather jacket mid-leap over a line of cars. Desmond looks at the photograph, then at the bike, then at Flo, who has peeled a shipping tag off the crate and is squinting at it.

DESMOND
Flo, who sent this?

FLO
It says "Harlan."

Max's hand stops on the cards. The room goes quiet. A small, amused crinkle appears at the corner of her eye, then spreads.

MAX
That son of a gun.

DESMOND
Who's Harlan?

MAX
Your grandfather's brother. He died in the spring.

DESMOND
Then who sent it?

MAX
He did, Desmond. He did. He always said he'd send me something nice when he got where he was going.

She gets up. She walks to the bike. Flo hurries to steady the oxygen tank on its wheels. Max lays her hand on the seat and strokes it with two fingers.

MAX (CONT'D)
Desmond. Fetch the helmet.

DESMOND
There's a helmet?

MAX
There's always a helmet.

He lifts the leather jacket and finds one beneath it. He holds it out. Max puts it on, buckles the strap, and swings a leg over the seat with a fluency that stops everyone's breath.

FLO
(quietly)
Miss Max.

MAX
Flo, if you tell Dr. Pruitt, I'll haunt you.

FLO
He'll see the tank.

MAX
Then I'll leave the tank.

She unhooks the oxygen tube, hands it to Flo, and grips the handlebars. Desmond covers his face with both hands. Flo stares at the tube.

The bike does not start. It doesn't have a key. Max grins, rests her forehead against the tank, and begins to laugh until the laughing turns to a wheeze, then a cough. Flo is at her side in an instant with the tube.

FLO (CONT'D)
Breathe, Miss Max.

MAX
(between breaths)
I am. I am. I'm breathing.

DESMOND
Nana.

MAX
Desmond, I want to sit here for a minute.

He pulls a chair beside the bike and sits. Flo sits on the other side. Max rests her hands on the handlebars, eyes closed, and for a while none of them says anything.